WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.532
Who gets paid?

2
00:00:01.986 --> 00:00:06.699
Beneficiary rules: primary and contingent, revocable and irrevocable,

3
00:00:06.723 --> 00:00:10.588
and what happens when the insured and the beneficiary die together.

4
00:00:11.529 --> 00:00:13.502
Beneficiaries are paid in order.

5
00:00:14.304 --> 00:00:18.192
The primary beneficiary is first in line to receive the proceeds.

6
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:23.804
If every primary beneficiary has died, the contingent, or secondary,

7
00:00:23.824 --> 00:00:26.157
beneficiary receives the money instead.

8
00:00:26.704 --> 00:00:29.965
And if no named beneficiary survives the insured,

9
00:00:30.024 --> 00:00:32.256
the proceeds go to the insured's estate,

10
00:00:32.276 --> 00:00:36.292
where they can be delayed by probate and exposed to creditors.

11
00:00:37.238 --> 00:00:39.327
Can the owner change the beneficiary?

12
00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:42.078
With a revocable designation, yes.

13
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:45.991
The owner can change it at any time, without anyone's permission.

14
00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:50.264
But an irrevocable beneficiary has vested rights.

15
00:00:50.555 --> 00:00:54.478
The owner needs that beneficiary's consent to change the designation,

16
00:00:54.607 --> 00:00:59.169
take a policy loan, assign the policy or surrender it.

17
00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:03.982
Now, what if the insured and the primary beneficiary die in the same accident,

18
00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:05.882
and no one can tell who died first?

19
00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:09.423
The law presumes the insured survived the beneficiary.

20
00:01:09.877 --> 00:01:12.604
So the primary is treated as dying first,

21
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.983
and the proceeds go to the contingent beneficiary,

22
00:01:16.344 --> 00:01:19.594
or to the insured's estate if there isn't one.

23
00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:23.889
This rule comes from the Uniform Simultaneous Death Act.

24
00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:29.380
One more rule: minors can't receive life insurance proceeds directly.

25
00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:33.060
The money has to go through a trust, a court-appointed guardian,

26
00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:36.624
or a custodian under the Uniform Transfers to Minors Act.

27
00:01:37.078 --> 00:01:40.386
Naming a trust as the beneficiary helps avoid delays.
